WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.139
Here is DP-CutMixSL step by step.

00:00:02.219 --> 00:00:09.806
A mixer draws mixing ratios lambda from a Dirichlet
distribution and builds a pseudorandom binary mask per client,

00:00:09.886 --> 00:00:13.371
with on-patches proportional to that client's lambda.

00:00:13.451 --> 00:00:18.146
Each client runs its input through the lower model
to get smashed data,

00:00:18.226 --> 00:00:20.433
masks it into Cutout smashed data,

00:00:20.513 --> 00:00:25.410
then adds white Gaussian noise for DP-Cutout
smashed data, and uploads it.

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:31.328
Because the masks are mutually exclusive and
collectively exhaustive at the patch level,

00:00:31.408 --> 00:00:38.053
the server simply adds the clients' noisy patches
into DP-CutMix smashed data with no blank patches,

00:00:38.133 --> 00:00:40.811
mixing labels by the same lambda weights.

00:00:40.891 --> 00:00:45.855
Forward and backward propagation then proceed as in
vanilla split learning.

00:00:45.935 --> 00:00:50.630
Crucially, only a noisy fraction of each client's
data is ever exposed.
